        <section id="the-different-terms-explained-in-our-example" >
          
            <h2>The different terms explained in our example</h2>
              <p>On pages 1 to 4 we are going to use a simple JavaScript program to demonstrate some of the concepts we are discussing.</p>
<p>Take a look at our <em>Javascript example</em> in the below activity which gives a brief overview on each of the terms and principles.  We will then go into more detail further on in the session.</p>


<div class="c-interactive" data-v2l-comp="1A9338FEE8584755AB48BB991EF417A3" data-v2l-interactive="uc/u2_s2_p1_js_overview" data-v2l-id="animation_container">
  <button data-v2l-loadbtn hidden>Begin Activity</button>
    <div data-v2l-canvas-alt>
    <h3>Variables</h3>
<p>A variable is used when it's content needs to change.  You would use a constant if an item needed to remain 'fixed'.</p>
<p>A variable is a container which can store a value.  A specific data type (e.g. a number) would be assigned to it which has the potential to alter during the running of a program.</p>
<p>So in our example, one of the variables is called <code>variable_1</code> and the number <code>25</code> has been assigned to it, and it has been declared using <code>var</code>.</p>
<h3>Comments</h3>
<p>Comments are very useful in coding as they can be used for several reasons:</p>
<ol>
<li>They can indicate what the piece of code does</li>
<li>They can provide information about bug fixes</li>
<li>They are useful when working in a team and other team members
need to know what a particular section of code does</li>
</ol>
<h3>Control flow: Conditional</h3>
<p>Control flow is the order in which a computer executes statements in a script. Code runs from the first line to the last unless the computer comes across structures like conditionals and loops which change the control flow.  An <code>if</code> statement would be used to specify a block of code to be executed if a certain condition is true, and <code>else</code> would be used if the same condition is false.</p>
<p>In our example the conditionals are <code>if</code> and <code>else</code> statements.  It is asking if the value of <code>variable_1</code> is equal to the value of <code>variable_2</code> - the outcome is dependent on if it is true or not.</p>
<h3>Control flow: Loop (Iteration)</h3>
<p>Iteration is the process of repeating sequential steps over and over (looping) in order to generate an intended outcome.</p>
<p>Each repetition of the process is a single iteration, and the outcome of each iteration forms the starting point for the next one.</p>
<p>Control flow - this statement would 'loop' until it reached the final value before moving to the next statement.</p>
<p>In our example a <code>for</code> statement has been used to 'loop' through the items of the variable <code>list_1</code> starting from first value.</p>
<p>The items <code>i</code>, <code>length</code> and <code>i++</code> determine how many times to 'loop' through the statement.</p>
<p>Each iteration will print the values of the variable until it reaches the final value.</p>
<h3>Function</h3>
<p>A 'function' is a block of code designed to perform a particular task.  It  can be used as many times as it is needed within a program.  This is useful as it means there is no need to repeat the same code.</p>
<p>In our example <code>myFunction</code> is returning the result of adding the values of the variables <code>input_1</code> to <code>input_2</code>.</p>
<h3>Control flow: Function call</h3>
<p>Once a function has been written it can be used as many times as it is needed within a program.  The code from within the function will execute when it is invoked 'called'.</p>
<p>Functions can be invoked from an event (e.g. a button is clicked), or it can be called from within the code - as it has been in our example.</p>
<p>'Control flow' - in our example, the code within our  function is executed (called) last.</p>
<p>You can see the result of this on page 4.</p>

    </div>
  </div>


          
